.TH LUKWM 1 "2026" "lukwm" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lukwm \- a minimal floating X11 window manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lukwm
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lukwm
is a minimal, floating window manager for X11, written in C and inspired by
.B sowm
and the suckless philosophy. It has no runtime configuration file; all
configuration is done by editing
.I config.h
and recompiling.
.PP
lukwm provides cascading window placement, 9 workspaces, focus-follows-mouse,
mouse-driven move/resize, fullscreen and gapped-maximize modes, partial EWMH
support (enough for status bars such as polybar to display workspace and
active window state), and recognition of dock and desktop window types so
that status bars and wallpaper managers are handled correctly instead of
being treated as regular windows.
.SH USAGE
lukwm takes no command-line arguments. It is normally started either from a
display manager (via the installed
.I lukwm.desktop
session file) or directly from
.BR startx (1):
.PP
.RS
startx lukwm \-\- :1 vt$(fgconsole)
.RE
.PP
The
.I vt$(fgconsole)
argument ensures Xorg is started on the VT you are actually logged into.
.SH CONFIGURATION
lukwm has no runtime config file or IPC mechanism for configuration. All
settings live in
.I config.h
in the source tree, which is compiled directly into the binary.
.PP
.I config.def.h
is the versioned template with default values.
.I config.h
is generated from it on first
.B make
and is never tracked by version control or overwritten by subsequent builds
\- it is where personal changes belong.
.PP
After editing
.IR config.h ,
run:
.RS
.nf
make
sudo make install
.fi
.RE
.PP
and either log out and back in, or use the in-session restart keybind
(default
.BR Super+Shift+R )
after rebuilding, to reload the running instance without ending the X
session.
.PP
Configurable values include the modifier keys, number of workspaces, border colors and width, gap and bar-height spacing used by
the maximize mode, the commands spawned by each keybind, and the list of
autostart commands.
.SH DEFAULT KEYBINDINGS
.TS
l l.
Super+Q	open terminal
Super+A	open application launcher
Super+X	open browser
Super+C	close focused window
Super+F	toggle fullscreen
Super+M	toggle maximize (gapped fullscreen)
Super+1..9	switch to workspace 1-9
Super+Shift+1..9	move focused window to workspace 1-9
Alt+Tab	cycle focus (current workspace)
Super+Shift+R	restart lukwm in place
Super+Left click+drag	move window
Super+Right click+drag	resize window
XF86AudioRaiseVolume	volume up
XF86AudioLowerVolume	volume down
XF86AudioMute	toggle mute
.TE
.PP
All keybindings are defined in
.I lukwm.c
and reference commands from
.IR config.h .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/lukwm/config.h
(or wherever the source tree lives) \- personal configuration, compiled into the binary.
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/lukwm
installed binary.
.TP
.I /usr/share/xsessions/lukwm.desktop
session file for display managers.
.SH LIMITATIONS
lukwm is a floating window manager only; it has no tiling layout engine.
Restarting the running instance (Super+Shift+R) resets in-memory window
tracking, so windows already open before a restart remain on screen but drop
out of Alt-Tab, workspace switching, and the EWMH active/client-list
properties until closed and reopened. lukwm currently targets a single
monitor. The number of simultaneously tracked windows is capped by
.B MAX_WINDOWS
in
.IR config.h .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR startx (1),
.BR Xorg (1)
.PP
